Abstract
The design of lower-cost alternative heterogeneous catalysts for the hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes using green method to synthesize the corresponding anilines is highly desirable. In this study, Ag was incorporated into the Co-MOFs during the growing process (Co-Ag(n)-MOFs), and then followed the carbothermal reduction process without any additional procedures, we synthesized a series of Co-Ag(n)@NCs. The self-supported catalysts exhibited excellent and stable catalytic performances for the chemoselective hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes without obvious dehalogenation. The Co-Ag bimetallic alloy nanoparticles were well-dispersed and protected from aggregation and leaching by the porous nitrogen doped carbon. Besides, either hydrazine hydrate (N\(_2\)H\(_4\)\(\cdot\)H\(_2\)O, generating byproducts N\(_2\) and H\(_2\)O) or H\(_2\) could be used as green reducing agent with excellent selectivity towards synthesizing the corresponding anilines. And when the Co/Ag content ratio was approximate 1:1, the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC showed the best catalytic performance. Moreover, the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC could be efficiently recovered by using an external magnetic force and reused
without obvious decrease of catalytic activity. Thus, such highly efficient, inexpensive, stable and magnetically recyclable catalysts could show great potentials in practical applications for many important reactions.
Keywords: Bimetallic alloy; Heterogeneous catalysts; Chemoselective; Halogenated nitrobenzenes
## 1. Introduction
Halogenated anilines and their derivatives, are of great importance in the production of modern industry, such as pharmaceuticals, pigments, dyes, agrochemicals, herbicides, polymers and other organic chemicals. [1-6] The hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes is one of the fundamental reactions for the synthesis of halogenated anilines. Traditionally, the preparation of halogenated anilines in industry is carried out by exploring reductants, for example, sulfides, Fe or Zn/HCl system. Nevertheless, there are many industry wastes including sulfide-bearing wastewater and solid wastes resulted from the traditional
methods, which are not environmentally friendly and cause selectivity problems. [7, 8] Because of the peculiarity of the environmental problems, hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\), or hydrazine hydrate \(\left(\mathrm{N}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{4} \cdot \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)\), only generating \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) as byproducts, could be used as suitable reductants for these reactions, which can reduce the negative impact on the environment and increase the purity of the resultant anilines. [4, 9-12] At present, the catalysts that have been developed for the hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes are mainly noble metal-based catalysts. [5, 6, 13-16] However, the noble metals are often lack of chemoselectivity attributed to their high catalytic activity, and they are usually further qualified with suitable additives or proper supports to improve their selectivity. What's more, the additives always bring about some environmental questions. Although supported gold nanoparticles (NPs) have created new chances in the design and manufacture of catalysts for the selective hydrogenation of nitrobenzenes, the risk in supply chains, and uneconomical prices of noble metals seriously limit the large-scale commercial applications. [17-20] And in practical terms, heterogeneous catalysts rather than homogeneous ones have many advantages in industrial applications, such
as simple separation process, recycling and reusing of catalysts. [3, 11, 21, 22] Therefore, intensive efforts have been made to design lower-cost alternative heterogeneous catalysts based on inexpensive, earth-abundant metals. [9, 11, 23-26]
To develop high-efficient and non-noble metal catalysts, the stability of the metal NPs has been one of the key questions. Because the NPs are inclined to agglomerate when treated without any protection measure in harsh conditions, thus the unique attributes derived from their small sizes may be decreased or lost. Furthermore, sintering or leaching of the base metals is also the main cause for the irreversible decreasing activity of the catalysts under the harsh environment. [11, 25, 27, 28] For example, Raney nickel as the bare metal catalyst without any external protection is in widespread use for the catalytic hydrogenation of nitrobenzenes to amines, but the disadvantages which include pyrophoric nature and moisture sensitivity restrict its practical application. [29, 30] Recently, the thermal decomposition of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) consisted of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands has been developed as a facile way to manufacture in-situ metal contained in porous carbon hybrid
materials. [12, 31-34] Most important is that the well-ordered metal ions regularly isolated by organic ligands could lead to the encapsulation of metal NPs within carbon. And the proper packaging structure presents to stabilize the metal NPs effectively, thus preventing them from leaching into the reaction liquid. On the other hand, bimetallic catalysts exhibit unique or improved properties which are distinct from those of their individual metal components, so numerous bimetallic catalytic materials have been widespread used in catalysis, such as Ni-Pd, Au-Ag, Pd-Ag, Pt-Pd etc. [35-41] Thus, utilizing MOFs as precursors or templates for the controllable fabrication of low cost functional bimetallic NPs embedded in the protective layer in large scale has gained significant attention in catalytic application. [42-44]
Based on the above considerations, in this effort, we introduced Ag into the Co based MOFs during the growing process, and Co-Ag bimetallic NPs were encapsulated into nitrogen doped carbon matrix (Co-Ag(n)@NC) through the carbothermal reduction process. The Co-Ag bimetallic NPs were partially wrapped or isolated by the nitrogen doped carbon. The unique structure could not only effectively protect the
metallic core from agglomeration and leaching in the harsh environment but also enhance the catalytic activity due to the synergistic effects of carbon materials and metallic particles. The resultant self-supported Co-Ag(n)@NC composites served as catalysts exhibited efficient and stable catalytic effects for the chemoselective hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes when using either hydrazine hydrate \(\mathrm{(N_{2}H_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}\), generating byproducts \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) as green reducing agent. Near full conversions of multiple functionalized nitrobenzenes towards synthesizing the corresponding anilines had been accomplished, especially the halogenated nitrobenzenes without obvious dehalogenation.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate \(\mathrm{(CoNO_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\) and silver nitrate \(\mathrm{(AgNO_{3})}\) were provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (China). 2-Methylimidazole (MeIM) was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. \(\mathrm{N_{2}H_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}\) (80 wt%) and \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) (≥97%) were
supplied by Chengdu Kelong chemical Co., Ltd. Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, M.W. = 30000) was supplied by Tianjin Guangfu fine chemical research institute. We acquired other chemicals from Tianjin Rionlon reagent Co., Ltd. All reagents were used as received.
## 2.2 Catalysts preparation
The resultant self-supported Co-Ag(n)@NC composites (where n indicated the content ratio of Co/Ag) were obtained by direct high temperature calcination of self-adjusted Co based MOFs by Ag incorporation (designated as Co-Ag(n)-MOFs). The preparation of Co-Ag(n)-MOFs were synthesized through the following solvothermal procedure: 12 mmol transition metal nitrate \(\left(\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2} \cdot 6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right.\), or \(\left.\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\right.\), or their mixture with different Co Ag molar ratio) were dissolved in 120 mL methanol, then 48 mmol MeIM was subsequently added into the formed solution. After completely mixing and stirring at room temperature, the precipitates were obtained through centrifugation and washed with methanol several times, and then dried completely in a vacuum oven, resulting in Co-Ag(n)-MOFs. A series of Co:Ag content
ratios (1:0, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, and 0:1) were designed. Then, the obtained powders with different contents of Co and Ag were calcined in a tubular furnace and further raised to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2h under Ar to generate the self-supported Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts.
For comparison purposes, various Co NPs, Ag NPs and bimetallic Co-Ag alloy NPs were also prepared. [45, 46] Firstly, 0.24 mmol transition metal nitrate \(\mathrm{(Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\), or \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\), or their mixture with different Co Ag molar ratio) were dissolved in \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and PVP (150 mg) was added as the protecting surfactant. The obtained solution was stirred for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\). Then, freshly prepared aqueous solution of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) (20 mg, 6 mL \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)) was added drop-wise under vigorous stirring to obtain a colloidal dispersion. The powder was obtained through centrifugation and washed with methanol several times, and then dried completely in a vacuum oven. Finally, the Co NPs, Ag NPs and bimetallic Co-Ag (2:1, 1:1, and 1:2) alloy were prepared.
## 2.3 Catalysts catalytic performances evaluation
2.3.1 Hydrogenation of nitrobenzenes use \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\) as the reductant
In one catalysis experiment, 0.5 mmol of nitrobenzenes, 5 mg of Co-Ag(n)@NC or metallic NPs catalysts, and 5 mL of green solvent ethanol were added into a dried round bottom flask and dispersed by ultrasonic treatment. Then 2 mmol of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added sequentially to the reaction mixtures, and then stirred under refluxing with a reflux condenser connected to the round bottom flask. The reaction results were evaluated by using gas chromatography-mass spectroscopy (GC-MS, Agilent 5977E).
## 2.3.2 Hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes use \(\mathbf{H}_{2}\) as the reductant
In a 100 mL stainless-steel autoclave the high-pressure hydrogenation reactions were performed. In one experiment, the autoclave was added with nitrobenzenes (0.5 mmol), Co-Ag(1:1)@NC (5 mg), and the green solvent ethanol (5 mL). The reaction system was well dispersed by
ultrasonic treatment. The sealed autoclave was purged with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) for three times. The reaction system was heated at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) and stirred at 700 rpm charged with 2 MPa of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). After the reaction, the remaining \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was carefully released and the reaction products were collected and filtered for further analysis by GC-MS (Agilent 5977E).
## 2.4 Characterizations
The prepared samples were characterized by a number of different analytic methods. Tecnai G2Tf20 transmission electronmicroscope (TEM) was utilized. Field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM, JSM-6701) was used to take SEM images. X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRD) patterns were performed (Rigaku D/max 2400 PC, Cu Kα). On Perkin-ElmerPHI-5702, the electronic properties of the samples surface were examined by X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS). Measuring \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption analysis at 77 K with a TriStar 3020 (Micromeritics), the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and pore-size distribution were generated. The element analysis was determined on a PerkinElmer EA2400 II instrument. The Co, Ag contents of the prepared samples were
determined by ICP-AES. GC-MS (Agilent 5977E) were employed in analysis and estimation of reaction results.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Catalyst characterization
The synthesis methods for Co based MOF-derived Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts generally employed Ag as an extra additive of effective precursor in one pot, and then with a second carbothermal reduction process under inert atmosphere. Here we proposed a host-guest chemistry strategy, during the construction of the ZIF-67 framework, \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)ions were introduced to the methanolic solution of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and MeIM to form a series of uniform Co-Ag(n)-MOFs. The possible formation process of Co-Ag NPs was proposed as follows: in the carbothermal reduction process of Co-Ag(n)-MOFs at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under inert atmosphere, the ligand MeIM was the direct carbon/nitrogen source and reducing agent, the coordinated Ag and Co metal ions could be co-reduced by carbon and the migrations between them would give metallic Co-Ag alloy NPs. [47-49] After the carbothermal reduction process, the Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts were
obtained. And the synthetic procedures were schematically illustrated in Fig. 1.
