## <insert Fig. 2>
The uniform morphology of the Co-Ag(n)-MOFs and Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts were tested by SEM and TEM. And the colours of the obtained powders with different Co and Ag ratios were almost the same purple or lavender, except the colour of the sample Co-Ag(0:1)-MOFs was white. For the representative Co-Ag(1:1)@NC, which showed the optimal reaction result in the hydrogenation of nitrobenzene. Therefore, Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was mostly discussed hereafter which derived from Co-Ag(1:1)-MOFs, and the SEM images of Co-Ag(1:0)-MOFs and Co-Ag(0:1)-MOFs were shown in Fig. S1. The rhombic dodecahedral shape structure became irregular with the increase of the adding quantity of Ag. As shown in Fig. 2a, the Co-Ag(1:1)-MOFs showed an analogous rhombic dodecahedral shape structure and a relative clean surface. Whereas after the high-temperature carbonization in inert atmosphere, the surface became rough and the uniform morphology was almost inherited,
as could be seen from Fig. 2b and 2c. TEM image Fig. 2d further illustrated that the uniform rhombic morphology was almost maintained. And Fig. 2e revealed the highly dispersed Co-Ag alloy NPs, which were partially or entirely coated and isolated by the nitrogen doped carbon. The unique structures could effectively protect the metallic core against the harsh reaction conditions and enhanced the catalytic activity due to the high surface area. The high resolution TEM (HR-TEM) images Fig. 2f showed the Co-Ag NPs were wrapped by the thin nitrogen doped carbon layers clearly, and the inset picture displayed the lattice fringes of the Co (111) planes (d = 0.20 nm) [11] and Ag (111) planes (d = 0.23 nm) [50]. High-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) and the corresponding element mapping analysis (Fig. 2g) demonstrated the well distribution of Co, Ag, C and N. It was clearly showed that the uniformly dispersed Ag species spatially distributed around with Co species in the whole catalyst matrix by the aid of the protection of the nitrogen doped carbon, which confirmed that the Co-Ag bimetallic alloy NPs protected by nitrogen doped carbon were successfully prepared.
## <insert Fig. 3>
XRD was measured to verify the composition and structural evolution of the prepared samples. The wide-angle XRD analysis patterns of the prepared samples Co-Ag(n)-MOFs (n = 1:0, 1:1 and 0:1) shown in Fig. S 2a confirmed their high crystallinity and similar zeolite-type structure. [43, 51] After the carbonization process, the obtained Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts were presented in Fig. 3. With the incorporation of Ag, the diffraction peaks of Ag appeared. In the XRD patterns of the carbonized Co-Ag(n)@NC, except for the Co-Ag(0:1)@NC, three main peaks at around 44.2, 51.5, and 75.8° appeared, corresponding to the (111), (200), and (220) planes, which showed the typical face-centered cubic (fcc) pattern of crystalline Co (JCPDS-15-0806). [11] The five main peaks at around 38.2, 44.4, 64.6, 77.6 and 81.7° corresponding to (111), (200), (220), (311), and (222) diffractions were ascribed to the fcc structure of Ag (JCPDS-87-0718). The Co-Ag(2:1)@NC, Co-Ag(1:1)@NC and Co-Ag(1:2)@NC showed both the peaks of crystalline Co and Ag. It's worth noting that the diffraction of Co peaks (200) planes slightly shift to lower value with the incorporation of Ag, which confirmed the successful
synthesis of Co-Ag alloy NPs and they were dispersed in the nitrogen doped carbon through the high temperature carbothermic reduction. [52]
## <insert Fig. 4>
The wide-scan XPS spectrum displayed that the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC mainly consisted of Co, Ag, C, N and O (Fig. 4a), which was consistent with the element mapping and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) results (Fig. S3). To further understand the surface valence states of the prepared samples, the fine spectrums were measured. Fig. 4b showed the C 1s high-resolution spectrum of the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC and the C 1s peaks could be fitted into three single peaks located at about 284.5, 285.6 and 289.3 eV, respectively. The strongest signal at 284.5 eV was due to sp<sup>2</sup> graphitic carbon, and the peak at ~285.6 eV could be associated with carbon nitrogen bond or carbon oxygen bond. Furthermore, the weak peak at ~289.3 eV could be mainly assigned to the O=C-N/O=C-O groups. [21, 32, 53, 54] The N 1s spectrum of the sample could be deconvoluted into five peaks centered at 398.5, 399.6, 400.5 401.3 and 402.3eV (nitrogen atoms in different environments, Fig. 4d), which were attributed to pyridinic N, amide or imine, pyrrolic N,
graphitic N and pyridine N-oxide, respectively (Fig. 4c). [55, 56] The contents of C and N were measured from elemental analysis and the results were shown in Table 1, which indicated the thin carbon layers and the small amount of N doping. The contained nitrogen groups could stabilize metallic particles to obtain well dispersed metallic particles effectively. And the unique thin nitrogen-doped carbon layer not only could effectively protect the metallic core from the harsh environment without affecting mass transfer process but also enhanced the catalytic reaction due to the synergistic effects of carbon materials and metallic particles.
The valence states of Co and Ag were also examined. As shown in the high resolution XPS spectrum of Co 2p (Fig. 4e), the binding energies in 778.3 eV and 793.3 eV could be attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}^{0}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{0}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), which were in accordance with the formation of metal Co. And the signals at 780.5 eV and 796.5 eV were assigned to Co (Ⅱ) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Co (Ⅱ) \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) [11, 33, 57, 58], which corresponded to the oxidized Co species and may come from the slight surface oxidation. The peaks observed in the XPS spectrum of Ag 3d (Fig. 4f) centered at 368 eV and 374 eV were
characteristic of metallic Ag [50, 59]. Moreover, the practical contents of Co and Ag obtained from ICP-AES measurement were shown in Table 1. The results were almost consistent with the content ratio of Co/Ag. And the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC showed the best catalytic performance as the Co/Ag content ratio of was approximate 1:1.
## <insert Fig. 5>
Typical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms and the pore size distribution of the samples Co-Ag(n)@NCs were shown in Fig. 5. In the \(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.4–0.8\) range, a hysteresis loop was observed, indicating a typical type-IV curve and revealing the existence of the uniform mesopores, which may derive from the carbonization of the precursor and the volatilization of volatile substances during the high-temperature carbonization process in inert atmosphere. [21, 60] In the catalytic reaction, the large number of mesopores seemed to play a certain function in the mass transfer. The BET specific surface areas and total pore volumes were displayed in Table 1. It showed that the BET surface areas and the pore volumes of the catalysts decreased with the increase of Ag addition, which could prove the successful incorporation of Ag as the
Co-Ag(0:1)@NC had the smallest specific surface area. The pore size of the tested materials which measured from the adsorption branches of the isotherms on account of the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda model was centered at about 4~6 nm (Table 1). Overall, the Ag was successfully incorporated with Co, and Co-Ag alloy NPs were well dispersed in the nitrogen doped carbon.
