## <insert Fig. 6>
Magnetic hysteresis loop of the sample Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was shown in Fig. 6. The saturation magnetization value of the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was about 44.2 emu g\(^{-1}\). In addition, the magnetization curves showed the magnetic hysteresis loops, which suggested magnetic nature of the catalyst. [4, 10] Therefore, the catalyst could be recovered simply and efficiently from the solution by utilizing an external magnetic force, which thus avoiding complex separation; and the collected catalysts could be reused for the next catalytic process.
## 3.2. Catalytic tests
In the catalytic test, the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was used to explore the optimized reaction temperature using 4-chloronitrobenzene as a model substrate and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) as the reducing agent. As it is known, ethanol is a relatively cheap, environmentally friendly medium. And due to its great solubility of nitro compounds, ethanol was chosen as the reaction solvent. [10, 11] The reaction test results were shown in Table 2. Unexpectedly, we found the existence of some aromatic azo compounds when the reaction results were estimated by using GC-MS. Adopting different reaction temperatures and sampling at the same time lengths, it was found that \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was a more suitable reaction temperature (Table 2, entry 2). When the reaction temperature was low, the catalyst exhibited relatively lower catalytic activity (Table 2, entry 1). But too high a temperature was, the catalytic results were also not good. Because the fast decomposition rate of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at a high temperature decreased the reaction contact time with the reactants (Table 2, entry 3). Moreover, the colour of the reaction solution became darker at high temperature. So for the consideration of saving energy and cost, the optimum reaction
temperature we selected was \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## <insert Table 2>
For further investigation, the prepared Co-Ag(n)@NC catalysts with different contents of Co and Ag were tested for the catalytic transfer hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene under the optimized reaction conditions (5 mg of catalyst, 0.5 mmol of 4-chloronitrobenzene, 2 mmol hydrazine hydrate, and 5mL ethanol, refluxed at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). Table 2, entry 2 and entry 4-7 summarized the catalytic results of using different catalysts, most of the catalysts displayed good catalytic performances. With the incorporation of Ag, from the catalytic results at the reaction time of \(1\,\mathrm{h}\), it showed that the catalytic activity became higher at first, and the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC exhibited the best catalytic performance, then gradually decreased. The Co-Ag(0:1)@NC only exhibited a conversion of \(5.26\%\) for the hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene without the addition of Co species (Table 2, entry 7), which demonstrated the great importance of the coexistence of Co and Ag in the nitrogen-doped carbon. In order to show the catalytic performances more directly, the volcano plot of the catalytic results of different catalysts at the reaction time of \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) was made
(Fig. 7), which clearly displayed that Co-Ag(1:1)@NC exhibited the best catalytic performance. The results demonstrated that the successful incorporation of Ag, forming Co-Ag alloy NPs, led to the greatly improved catalytic performance of the hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene. When the 4-chloronitrobenzene was hydrogenated without the reductant \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), the reaction almost did not occur (Table 2, entry 8). It also showed no reaction without a catalyst, and the only addition of the reductant \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Table 2, entry 9). Thus, from the above investigation, the representative Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was chosen for the further reaction investigation, which showed the optimal reaction results.
