## <insert Fig. 8>
Kinetic study for the reduction of 4-chloronitrobenzene using Co:Ag(1:1)@NC was then studied. In order to eliminate the effect of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) on the kinetic study, the usage of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was enlarged to 10 mmol. Time course of the transfer hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene was shown in Fig. 8a. Because \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was used
in excess compared to 4-chloronitrobenzene, its concentration in the reaction solution could be considered to be constant. Therefore, a pseudo first-order reaction could be applied to evaluate the kinetics of this reaction. [6, 8, 15] The apparent rate constant \( k \) could be counted by the equation: \( -\ln(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathbf{C}_{0}) = k \cdot t \), where \( \mathbf{C}_{0} \) was the initial concentration of 4-chloronitrobenzene, and \( \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}} \) was the concentration of 4-chloronitrobenzene at reaction time \( t \) (min). As shown in Fig. 8b, the values of \( k \) calculated from the linear slope of \( -\ln(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathbf{C}_{0}) \) versus \( t \) was \( 0.0757 \, \mathrm{min}^{-1} \), which showed a relatively good reaction rate.
## <insert Table 3>
For comparison purposes, various Co NPs, Ag NPs and bimetallic Co-Ag (2:1, 1:1, and 1:2) alloy samples were also tested for the catalytic hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene, and the results were summarized in Table 3. The hydrogenation using various mono- and bimetallic NPs showed similar catalytic results compared with \( \mathrm{Co-Ag(n)}@\mathrm{NC} \), as the Co-Ag (1:1) alloy exhibited comparatively good catalytic performance among the NPs. Nevertheless, the catalytic performances of the NPs were not good when the reaction time was prolonged. The reason was probably
because that the reducibility of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \cdot \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and the relatively high reaction temperature led to the agglomeration of the pure metal NPs without a protective layer, which confirmed the importance of the protective effects of the nitrogen doped carbon layer from the other side.
## <insert Table 4>
Inspired by the good catalytic activity of the prepared Co-Ag(1:1)@NC, the catalyst was examined for the hydrogenation of various functionalized nitroarenes especially the halogenated nitrobenzenes to the corresponding anilines. Table 4 summarized the results obtained from the catalytic reduction of the substituted nitrobenzenes. From Table 4, entry 1-7, it indicated that the catalyst had good catalytic activity for a wide range of halogenated nitrobenzenes with high conversion and without obvious dehalogenation. The hydrogenation of various functionalized nitrobenzenes using the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC also obtained the corresponding anilines with high conversion and excellent selectivity, as shown in Table 4, entry 8-15. The catalyst exhibited considerable catalytic activity and good selectivity, which may demonstrate the potential industrial applications of the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC for the
chemoselective hydrogenation of nitrobenzenes. What's more, the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC was also investigated for the hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) as the reducing agent (Table 5). The Co-Ag(1:1)@NC also exhibited good catalytic results, the halogenated nitrobenzenes could be hydrogenated to the corresponding anilines with high yield and excellent selectivity nearly 100% without obvious dehalogenation.
## <insert Table 5>
The stability and the reusability are essential factors in robust heterogeneous catalysts. Thus, the recyclability of the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC catalyst was further evaluated. After each hydrogenation reaction, the catalyst was recovered from the reaction solution through the use of external magnetic fields, then washed with absolute ethanol, and dried in a vacuum oven at ambient temperature for the next catalytic test. As shown in Fig. 9, the conversion was remained well after five successive cycles, revealing its high stability and good catalytic activity. And the recycled catalyst was further characterized, the XRD pattern (Fig. S 2b) confirmed that the crystalline phase and structure of the reused catalyst
did not change much.
