## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, a series of Co-Ag(n)@NCs was synthesized through a facile solvothermal procedure and the carbothermal reduction. The chemical doping process was the key to the formation of possible Co-Ag-MOFs complex connected into 3D organic framework nanocrystals. The Co-Ag bimetallic NPs were well dispersed and protected by the porous nitrogen doped carbon by subsequent thermal activation. With the proper incorporation of Ag into Co, the catalytic activity was greatly enhanced. When the Co/Ag content ratio was approximate 1:1, the Co-Ag(1:1)@NC exhibited the optimal catalytic results for the hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes without obvious dehalogenation using either \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) as the green reductant. The possible synergistic effects between Co-Ag bimetallic NPs and nitrogen-doped carbon might be responsible for the enhanced activity. Therefore, this study would offer certain enlightenment for the
constructing of bimetallic nanostructures protected by nitrogen-doped carbon. And these highly efficient, inexpensive, stable and magnetically recyclable catalysts showed great potential in practical applications for many important industrial reactions.
